---

<BayPHL1
  title="BayPHL 1"
  tabs={[
    { href: "#info", label: "Summary" },
    { href: "#puzzles", label: "Puzzles" },
    { href: "#credits", label: "Credits" },
  ]}
>
  <Block>
    <a href="/"><strong>← Back to BayPHL homepage</strong></a>
  </Block>

  <Block id="info">
    <h1 class="font-black font-roboto-slab text-4xl mb-4">BayPHL 1</h1>

    <p>
      BayPHL 1 took place on November 8th and 9th, 2025 in the Marina District
      of San Francisco. A total of 75 teams participated. Thanks to everyone who
      joined us, and a special congratulations to the teams that finished:

      <details style="margin-bottom: 1em;">
        <summary>Finishing teams (in alphabetical order)</summary>

        <ul class="list-disc list-inside mt-4 ml-4">
          {finishers.map((team) => <li>{team}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </details>
    </p>

    <p>
      <em
        >(Did you finish BayPHL 1 but aren’t credited above? Email us and let us
        know at <a href="mailto:bayphl1@bayphl.org">bayphl1@bayphl.org</a>. We
        did our best but the beer may have distracted us a little from marking
        things off in our tracker.)</em
      >
    </p>
  </Block>

  <Block id="puzzles">
    <h2
      class="font-bold font-roboto-slab text-2xl border-b-black border-b mb-4"
    >
      Puzzles and Solutions
    </h2>

    <p>
      BayPHL 1 was an adaptation of <a href="https://24.baphl.org">BAPHL 24</a> for
      the San Francisco Bay Area. As with BAPHL, teams of newly minted archaeologists
      were sent out to investigate well-known wonders of the world and see if they
      could unearth any secrets (Beginner Archaeologists — Yay! — Pursuing Historical
      Landmarks). Starting in Fort Mason, teams traversed the Marina District of
      San Francisco to the Palace of the Fine Arts, where they solved their first
      metapuzzle and learned their first big secret. That enabled them to return
      to sites they had already visited, discovering new wonders along the way, before
      ending at Ghirardelli Square, where they received their final metapuzzle.
    </p>

    <p>
      BayPHL 1 consisted of 21 puzzles, broken into two rounds. The first round
      included 6 “major wonders” and 6 “minor wonders,” (puzzles which leveraged
      specific locations, physical components, or temporary installations),
      capped off by a metapuzzle. The second round featured 7 additional puzzles
      and a capstone metapuzzle.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here are the puzzles from BayPHL 1, grouped into the stops where teams
      collected them:
    </p>

    <ul class="mb-4">
      <li><a href={packet1}>Packet 1: Start</a></li>
      <li><a href={packet2}>Packet 2: Beta</a></li>
      <li><a href={packet3}>Packet 3: Gamma</a></li>
      <li><a href={packet4}>Packet 4: Beta Revisited</a></li>
      <li><a href={packet5}>Packet 5: Alpha Revisited</a></li>
      <li><a href={packet6}>Packet 6: End</a></li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Most of the puzzles were taken directly from BAPHL 24, occasionally with
      very light modifications (for instance, eliminating a clue about
      “Camber-ville” which might not land this far away from Cambridge). For
      BAPHL, we offered teams the option signing up for either “normal” or
      “hard” difficulty. As a logistical optimization for BayPHL, we only
      produced a single difficulty which blended the two modes. Rather than
      reproduce the solutions for those puzzles, <a href="https://24.baphl.org"
        >check out the BAPHL 24 solutions</a
      >.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here are the solutions for the two puzzles that we created for BayPHL 1
      (replacing “Young Kid Yoga: A Park Day” and ”Field Trip” from BAPHL):
    </p>

    <table class="border-collapse w-full mb-4">
      <tr>
        <td class="w-[40%]">The Lost Books</td>
        <td class="w-[40%]">Evan Broder, James Douberley</td>
        <td class="w-[10%] text-right">
          <a href={puzzleTheLostBooks} target="_blank">Puzzle</a>
        </td>
        <td class="w-[10%] text-right">
          <a href="/1/puzzles/the-lost-books/solution">Solution</a>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="w-[40%]">Supply Chains</td>
        <td class="w-[40%]">Jessen Yu</td>
        <td class="w-[10%] text-right">
          <a href={puzzleSupplyChains} target="_blank">Puzzle</a>
        </td>
        <td class="w-[10%] text-right"
          ><a href="./puzzles/supply-chains/solution.html">Solution</a></td
        >
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="text-sm italic" colspan="4">
          Supply Chains was a location puzzle, which required teams to find the <a
            href="https://www.atlasobscura.com/places/world-sources-of-food-mural"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">World Sources of Food mosaic</a
          > on the side of the Safeway on Marina Boulevard.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </table>
  </Block>

